# Continuous Integration

The gate is [`.github/workflows/ci.yml`](../../../.github/workflows/ci.yml); read it for the step list. It is both the PR check and a reusable workflow: `release.yml`'s `gate` job calls it, so every push to `main` is validated before a tag is cut ([release.mdc](release.mdc)). Deploy: [cd.mdc](cd.mdc). This rule loads with every workflow, so its expression-context, script, and hardening rules apply to all of them (install and telemetry: where a workflow installs or runs the tools).

## Expression contexts - read before touching `env:`

Workflow-level `env:` may reference only `github`, `secrets`, `inputs`, `vars`; job-level `env:` adds `needs`, `strategy`, `matrix`; `runner`, `job`, `steps`, and `env` are step-level only. An unavailable context is a **validation error**: the file stops parsing, GitHub reports the run under the file path instead of the workflow name, and zero jobs execute - it never degrades to an empty string (this once left `ci.yml` and `cd.yml` unparseable for 14 pushes). Anything needing `$RUNNER_TEMP` goes through a step:

```yaml
- run: echo "NODE_COMPILE_CACHE=$RUNNER_TEMP/node-compile-cache" >> "$GITHUB_ENV"
```

## Invariants

- **Triggers**: `pull_request`, `workflow_call`, `workflow_dispatch`. No `push:` - `release.yml`'s `gate` covers pushes to `main`, so a commit is never checked twice.
- **The required check is the job name** `Lint, typecheck, test, and build` (the `main` ruleset, strict up-to-date): renaming the `ci` job leaves every PR waiting on a check that never reports.
- **Parity is about the kinds of checks, not the mechanism**: change both sides when adding or renaming a gate (`deps:format:check` exists because both call it by name). Locally `pnpm run ci` is `pnpm run types`, `pnpm run boundaries`, one `turbo run` of the `//#` root tasks plus `check-types test build deploy:check` with `--continue=dependencies-successful`, then `pnpm run audit`; here the same checks are separate `parallel:` steps calling bare `pnpm run` scripts. The local `&&` chain is deliberately serial (`types` before type-aware lint, `boundaries` a cheap fail-fast pre-gate, `audit` a success-gated network tail); a parallel-sections rewrite was rejected to keep the gate zero-dependency. Never route the workflow through the `//#` tasks: a root task's default inputs span the repo, so under `futureFlags.affectedUsingTaskInputs` it would always be affected and defeat `--affected`.
- **Scope by event**: `--affected` on `pull_request` only, the full graph on `workflow_call` / `workflow_dispatch` (a release validates everything), expressed as two `if:`-gated steps - never interpolate the flag into a `run:` body. The dependency audit is `pull_request`-only for the inverse reason: the advisory DB changes daily, so an auditing release gate is not a pure function of the commit (local `pnpm run ci` still audits).
- **The Changesets step is `pull_request`-only**, like the audit: `release:check` diffs against `origin/main`, so the release gate has nothing to check. Locally `ci`, `ci:agent`, and `ci:sandbox` run it as `//#release:check`; `ci:affected` does not, since mid-task a changeset is not due yet. `CHANGESET_ALLOW_MAJOR` reads the `release:major` label from the event payload, and label events start no CI run, so adding the label takes a re-run of the job. The step also gets `GITHUB_TOKEN` (the job's `contents: read`) to read the base `pnpm-workspace.yaml` through the API. `CHANGESET_SKIP_RUNTIME_DEPENDENCIES` is set only on a Dependabot PR when `CHANGESET_BOT_TOKEN` is absent ([release.mdc](release.mdc)).
- **No `TURBO_SCM_BASE`**: on `pull_request` turbo infers the base from GitHub Actions, and `futureFlags.githubActionsRemoteBaseRefFallback` resolves it to `origin/<base_ref>` (checkout creates only remote-tracking refs). Never set it off PRs: `github.event.before` is all-zeros on a new branch, which silently degrades `--affected` to "everything changed". Keep `fetch-depth: 0` + `filter: blob:none`.
- **One job**, with the `parallel:` step keyword (GA 2026-06-25, alongside `background` / `wait` / `wait-all` / `cancel`) for OXC (`lint:ci`, pinned to `--format=github` for PR annotations, and `format`), `boundaries`, `syncpack`, and zizmor; Knip is diagnostic only, never a step here or in `pnpm run ci` ([quality-report.mdc](quality-report.mdc)). A `parallel:` list item takes **no sibling keys**. At most 10 background steps run at once and a PR runs 9, so an eleventh item would queue behind the others. The "Generate cf types" step (`pnpm run types`) runs before `parallel:`: type-aware lint needs the gitignored types, and the parallel turbo step, their only other producer, is `--affected` on PRs. There is no types-drift step (nothing generated is committed). `check-types test build deploy:check` is a **single** `turbo run --continue=dependencies-successful` at default concurrency (no override in `turbo.json`), so one run reports every package's failures.
- **The deploy dry-run is the `deploy:check` task** (`cf deploy --prebuilt --mode production --dry-run` per app) inside that single run, never a step after the join: `turbo run deploy -- --dry-run` hashes the pass-through args into every task of the graph, so the whole app build re-ran uncached. It depends on `build` only, and with `--prebuilt` needs neither token nor account ID, so the job gets no Cloudflare variables. `ci:sandbox` drops it with `build`: cf also stats the app's `.env`, which the agent sandbox denies (EPERM), and `SKIP_ENV_FILES` does not reach `cf deploy`.
- **The release PR's check comes from a dispatched run**: GitHub holds the `pull_request` runs of a `github-actions[bot]` PR at `action_required` until a writer approves them, so `release.yml`'s `version` job runs `gh workflow run ci.yml --ref changeset-release/main`; a dispatch is exempt, and its run reports the bare job name on the PR head. The job-level `if` still skips an approved `pull_request` run of that PR, whose `release:check` would fail on the changesets the PR consumes. It is bound to `head_ref == 'changeset-release/main'` **and** author `github-actions[bot]`: a prefix match would let any PR skip the required check by naming its branch, and a `pull_request` `branches-ignore` filters the *base* branch, so it cannot express this.
- **Step bodies longer than a few lines are scripts** under `.github/actions/<concern>/` (`cd/`, `changesets/`, `previews/`, `quality/`, `release/`; shared modules in `lib/`), not inline YAML. Invoke with `bash <path>`, wire inputs through step-level `env:`, open each script with `set -euo pipefail`, and fail fast on missing input (`:?` guards or an explicit check). Short steps stay inline.
- **Install, in every workflow that installs**: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` after `pnpm/setup` with `install: false`; Node `runtime: node@24`, matching root engines.
- **Only `ci.yml` saves the pnpm store cache**: `actions/cache` keyed on `hashFiles('pnpm-lock.yaml')`, never `pnpm/setup`'s `cache: true`, whose key includes the run id and saved a new ~300 MB entry on every job. `quality.yml`'s `analyze` job and `changeset-pr-status.yml`'s `pr-status` job restore the same key read-only with `actions/cache/restore` ([quality-report.mdc](quality-report.mdc)). Jobs holding a write token or Cloudflare credentials (`version`, CD, Previews) restore no cache: defense in depth, since cache scoping already keeps PR entries away from `main`.
- **Remote cache**: job env wires `TURBO_TOKEN`, `TURBO_TEAM`, and `TURBO_REMOTE_CACHE_SIGNATURE_KEY` (repo secret, >= 32 bytes, the same value as local machines; `turbo.json` enables `remoteCache.signature` + `longerSignatureKey`). `workflow_call` declares those secrets explicitly so the release gate never receives Cloudflare credentials; never `secrets: inherit`. `TURBO_CACHE` is `local:rw,remote:rw` on `pull_request` and `local:rw,remote:w` everywhere else: signing checks integrity, not trust, and same-repo PR runs hold both token and key, so a PR could upload an entry the release gate would replay as a passing `check-types` / `test` / `build`; write-only makes the gate run the full graph fresh and publish trusted artifacts. CD has no Turbo token at all. `envMode` is `strict`: a workflow `env:` value reaches a turbo task only if `turbo.json` lists it in `env` / `passThroughEnv`.
- **Hardening, every workflow**: every `uses:` is a full-length commit SHA with a `# vX.Y.Z` comment; `actions/checkout` sets `persist-credentials: false`; workflow `permissions: {}` with per-job re-grants (`ci.yml`: `contents: read` only - artifact upload and `actions/cache` use the runner's runtime token, not `GITHUB_TOKEN`); a same-repo `uses:` is `$/<path>` (this repository at the running commit, no checkout needed), never `./<path>` - Dependabot ignores both; runners `ubuntu-24.04`, never `ubuntu-latest`. A workflow that runs turbo, cf, or Wrangler turns telemetry off at workflow `env:` (`DO_NOT_TRACK` covers cf; `CF_SEND_TELEMETRY: "false"` beside `WRANGLER_SEND_METRICS`).
- `cancel-in-progress` only on `pull_request`. Never interpolate `github.ref_name` into script bodies. No production or Cloudflare secrets in this workflow (CD only).
- Never add scanners "for completeness" without a concrete threat and owner. zizmor (the `Workflow security` step) has both: workflow edits, often agent-written, that bring an unpinned or impostor pin, a `# vX.Y.Z` comment not matching its SHA, template injection, or permission creep; the owner is the `/.github/` CODEOWNERS entry. It is `pull_request`-only like the audit, since its online audits query the GitHub API, and CI-only (no Node distribution): reproduce with `uvx zizmor .` when uv is available. Fix a finding; an unavoidable one gets an inline `# zizmor: ignore[<audit>]` naming why.
